    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Kearsarge was named after the USS Kearsarge (1861) by a former naval officer who worked for Calumet and Hecla Mining Company.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        3,
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kearsarge%2C_Michigan",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The community first settled in 1867, and a post office opened on November 20, 1893 with Barnett S. Shearer as first postmaster.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        14,
        15,
        28,
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://kids.kiddle.co/Kearsarge,_Michigan",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "A train station on the Hancock and Calumet Railroad served Kearsarge, later becoming part of the Duluth, South Shore and Atlantic Railway.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        4,
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kearsarge%2C_Michigan",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "From 1882 to 1925, copper miners extracted an amygdaloid copper lode, a rare rock type prized for its copper content.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13,
        27,
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kearsarge%2C_Michigan",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "During the Great Depression, WPA workers built a stone memorial to the 1862 naval ship, though it bears little resemblance to the sloop it commemorates.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kearsarge%2C_Michigan",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Kearsarge lies about 2 miles northeast of Calumet/Laurium along U.S. Route 41/M-26, a key corridor in Houghton County.",
